    "result_text": "Done. The `locality` derivation is implemented and the full suite (28 tests) is green.\n\n## What I changed\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `locality` property to the `Owner` response schema (so `OwnerDto` is generated with the field).\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added a read-time derivation on `toOwnerDto`:\n   ```java\n   @Mapping(target = \"locality\",\n       expression = \"java(org.springframework.samples.petclinic.mapper.Locality.of(owner.getCity()))\")\n   ```\n   This mirrors the existing derived fields (`displayName`, `initials`, `membershipTier`).\n\n3. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/Locality.java`** (new) \u2014 holds the fixed table `Sydney\u2192NSW, Melbourne\u2192VIC, Brisbane\u2192QLD` and returns `\"UNKNOWN\"` for anything else (including null).\n\n## One trap worth flagging\n\nMy first attempt put the `of(String)\u2192String` helper *inside* the `OwnerMapper` interface. MapStruct treats any single-arg method whose return type matches a target property as an implicit type-conversion method and applied it to **every** String field on the create path \u2014 telephone became `\"UNKNOWN\"`, which then failed the `Owner` entity's E.164 `@Pattern` at persist time and turned every successful create into a 500. That surfaced as failures in `Cp16Tests`/`Cp17Tests` on `POST /api/owners`, not on the GET I was targeting.\n\nMoving the helper into a standalone `Locality` class fixed it (confirmed by reading the generated `OwnerMapperImpl.java` \u2014 `toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)` now maps fields straight through). I saved this pitfall to memory since it's easy to hit again here.\n\nVerification: `./mvnw test` \u2192 **28 tests, 0 failures, BUILD SUCCESS**, including the new `Cp17Tests` asserting `locality` is `\"NSW\"` for Sydney and `\"UNKNOWN\"` for an unknown city.",
